<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Android counterpart of the NSAppTransportSecurity block in ios/GritChat/Info.plist.

  Same posture as iOS, for the same reason: the dev relay speaks plain ws://, and Android's cleartext
  policy governs ws:// exactly as it governs http://, so the hosts the relay actually listens on need
  naming. iOS never set NSAllowsArbitraryLoads; this file never sets a permissive base config. A
  build that trusts every host cannot tell a misconfigured relay from a hijacked one.

  What each entry is for:
    10.0.2.2  the Android emulator's alias for the host Mac's loopback, so an emulator reaches a
              hop-relayd on the workstation, and reaches Metro on 8081.
    localhost
    127.0.0.1 a physical phone with `adb reverse tcp:18765 tcp:18765` (and tcp:8081 for Metro), plus
              the Detox instrumentation's own websocket to its test server.

  PHYSICAL PHONE RUNS: a phone dialling the workstation directly rather than through adb reverse
  needs the Mac's LAN address, measured with `ipconfig getifaddr en0`, added below as its own
  <domain> inside this <domain-config>, and iOS needs the same address as a named
  NSExceptionDomains entry. Nothing about that changes the base config. ADD IT FOR THE RUN AND
  REMOVE IT BEFORE COMMITTING. A developer's private address checked in here is not a convenience,
  it is a cleartext exception shipped to every user for a host none of them can reach, and it
  discloses the address besides. One was committed here and in the iOS plist and has now been
  removed.

  Cleartext is not the only gate on that path. On Android 17 (SDK 37) the OS also gates
  local-network addresses, 10.0.2.2 and a LAN IP alike, behind ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK. React Native's
  own debug manifest declares it and requests it at runtime for Metro's sake, so a debug build gets
  it for free and the relay rides along; LocalNetworkPermissionUtil never prompts in a release
  build. So a release build pointed at a LAN relay on such a device fails whatever this file says,
  and that is the right trade: the app is not declaring a permission that production, which dials
  wss:// over the internet, has no use for. Proof runs are debug builds.

  This file's whole scope is development. Nothing here exempts a production relay, which is wss://
  and needs no exemption at all.
-->
<network-security-config>
  <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />

  <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
    <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
  </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
